    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_da89ea05632cb5daf63056611d4b319d2ec07d8c9efcc3f5acccd7a4e55a7a71",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Rapid cusp growth removes tachyonic partition functions from the proved Rankin-Selberg-Zagier regime, but a conjectural decomposition could isolate a rapidly growing automorphic summand whose constant Fourier mode vanishes and leave a moderate remainder to which the theorem applies. The two-dimensional heterotic j-subtraction realizes the proposed mechanism in a restricted completion class, while the ten-dimensional example retains only an unambiguous constant-mode consequence when the global completion cannot be identified.",
